            "summary": "This article describes a significant case of severe contact dermatitis experienced by a 60-year-old woman who used Cortivex Cream for treating eczema. The woman developed a serious skin reaction within a day of applying the cream, which included intense redness, swelling, and blistering beyond the application area. After stopping the use of the cream, the woman's symptoms resolved after 10 days with the help of topical corticosteroids. Further investigation revealed that the cream was from a batch that had been recalled due to a manufacturing defect. \n\nThis document is relevant to patient safety because it highlights a serious adverse reaction associated with a pharmaceutical product, indicating potential quality issues. It raises concerns about the safety of the specific batch of Cortivex Cream and emphasizes the importance of monitoring products for defects that can lead to harmful effects on patients. Additionally, it showcases the need for reporting and addressing manufacturing errors to prevent similar incidents in the future.",
